##-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## SpyGlass CDC Waiver File
##-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## CDC waivers require extra care — an incorrect CDC waiver can mask
## a real metastability bug that causes silicon failure.
##
## Approval process for CDC waivers:
##   1. Engineer documents justification with timing analysis
##   2. Peer review by another CDC-experienced engineer
##   3. Sign-off by design lead or verification lead
##   4. Record waiver in design review meeting minutes
##
## Types of legitimate CDC waivers:
##   - Quasi-static signals (change only during reset/configuration)
##   - Test/scan-mode signals (only active in non-functional mode)
##   - Signals protected by external protocol (documented handshake)
##   - False paths (signals that physically cannot cause issues)
##-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

##=============================================================================
## Example CDC Waivers
##=============================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 1: Quasi-static configuration signals
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Configuration registers are written once during boot-up while the
## design is held in reset. They remain stable during all functional
## operation. No synchronizer is needed because the signal never
## toggles while clocks are running.
##
## Evidence: Boot sequence holds rst_n low for >100 clock cycles after
## configuration writes complete. All downstream logic is in reset
## during configuration.
##
## waive -rule {Ac_cdc01}
##   -from {u_config/cfg_mode_reg[*]}
##   -to {u_core/cfg_mode_sync[*]}
##   -comment "Quasi-static: written once during boot while design is in reset"

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 2: Scan/test mode signals
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## scan_enable and scan_mode signals cross clock domains but are only
## active during manufacturing test (scan mode). During functional
## operation, these signals are tied to constant values.
##
## waive -rule {Ac_cdc01}
##   -from {scan_enable}
##   -to {*}
##   -comment "Test-mode signal; constant during functional operation"

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 3: Synchronized externally by protocol
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## In some cases, CDC synchronization is handled at a higher level
## (e.g., a software handshake protocol ensures the signal is stable
## for N cycles before the receiver reads it). Document the protocol.
##
## waive -rule {Ac_cdc02}
##   -from {u_dma/descriptor_addr[*]}
##   -to {u_engine/desc_addr_reg[*]}
##   -comment "SW protocol: descriptor written, then doorbell ring. HW reads
##             after doorbell sync, by which time data has been stable >10 cycles."

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 4: Reset crossing (handled by reset synchronizer)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## If SpyGlass reports Ac_unsync_rst but you have a reset synchronizer
## that SpyGlass doesn't recognize (e.g., a custom library cell), you
## may need to waive and document.
##
## waive -rule {Ac_unsync_rst}
##   -from {u_rst_gen/async_rst_n}
##   -to {u_core/*}
##   -comment "Reset synchronized by u_rst_sync (custom library cell RSTSYNC2)"

##=============================================================================
## Template for CDC Waivers
##=============================================================================
## waive -rule {Ac_cdc0X}
##   -from {source_instance/signal}
##   -to {destination_instance/signal}
##   -comment "Justification: <explain why this crossing is safe>"
##
## Required documentation:
##   - What is the signal?
##   - Why doesn't it need synchronization?
##   - What guarantees stability? (protocol, timing, quasi-static)
##   - Who reviewed and approved this waiver?
